 }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (2+3b) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+3b}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we're told that $\\overset{―}{x}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{2+3b}{5} \\\\ 50 & =2+3b \\\\ 48 & =3b \\\\ b & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-160026/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b346e7e1-b641-5a97-9677-84a0ff6ab2dc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160026"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-86567"] :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=5$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} & =9+5+a+a+2 \\\\  & =16+2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is given by the expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (16+2a) \\\\  & =\\frac{16+2a}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we're told that $\\overset{―}{x}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =\\frac{16+2a}{5} \\\\ 30 & =16+2a \\\\ 14 & =2a \\\\ a & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
